From fdfd9822d3de7acc416fb7138943751b487f0d0b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sebastian Werner <swernerx@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 19:03:25 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] perf(parser): measure each container line's indentation and
 end once
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The list item collector and the block quote walk asked the same
questions of every line they consumed — where it ends, where the next
one starts, how deeply it is indented, whether it is blank, and what
follows that indentation — and every answer came from its own scan over
the same bytes. An indented list continuation line was walked by the
blank test, by `calc_indentation` and by `push_line_without_indent`, and
walked again by `trim_start` or `first_non_whitespace_in_line` once it
turned out to carry a marker or to continue a paragraph lazily; a quote
line walked its prefix for the blank test and again for the marker's
column arithmetic.

`LineFacts` now answers all of that from one walk of the leading
space/tab run followed by one terminator search that starts where the
run ends. `push_measured_line_without_indent` dedents a tab-free run by
slicing, and `line_starts_block` takes the first content byte its
callers have already located.

This is the round-4 `lines` candidate ported onto the byte-level
whitespace helpers, with the block quote regression it measured fixed:
the facts used to search for the terminator before anything else, so the
blank line that closes a quote paid a search the old walk never made. The
quote now measures the run, stops on a blank line from the byte after
it, and only then looks for the line's end. The list walk needs every
line's end, blank ones included, so it had no such early exit to lose.

The result is unchanged because every fact is defined as the helper it
replaces: `trimmed` and `is_blank` are `whitespace::trim_start` and
`whitespace::is_blank` of the line, so a no-break-space line stays
content, and the two column counts stay apart because
`calc_indentation`, which list continuation depth is compared against,
charges a tab a flat four columns. The walks they replaced are kept
verbatim under `cfg(test)`, and a differential test parses generated
container soup, the specification fixtures and the frozen corpus through
both, in LF, CRLF and CR, and requires the same AST, source spans
included, and the same HTML.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
